# Why pay a chief executive so much?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What is the principal–agent problem? Do incentive contracts align interests or just raise pay?"
next: {"title":"Why do incentive schemes backfire?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/116-why-do-incentive-schemes-backfire.cast"}

## From 21 to 1 to 290 to 1
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 1965
      - 1973
      - 1978
      - 1989
      - 1995
      - 2000
      - 2007
      - 2009
      - 2021
      - 2022
      - 2023
    series:
      - {name: CEO ÷ worker, color: '#b5482e', points: true, values: [21, 23, 31, 60, 118, 384, 330, 167, 405, 360, 290]}
      - {name: to 1978, color: '#b5482e', points: true, values: [21, 23, 31, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null]}
    stage: 0
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 450
    xlim:
      - 1965
      - 2023
    y_label: CEO pay ÷ worker pay
    x_label: Year
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 90
      w: 640
      h: 540
sources:
    - id: epi2024
      title: 'CEO pay declined in 2023: But it has soared 1,085% since 1978 compared with a 24% rise in typical workers'' pay'
      authors: Josh Bivens, Elise Gould, Jori Kandra (Economic Policy Institute)
      year: 2024
      finding: 'Realized CEO-to-worker compensation ratio, top 350 US firms: 20.8 (1965), 31.0 (1978), 60.5 (1989), 384.1 (2000), about 405 (2021), 290.3 (2023); average realized CEO pay $22.2m in 2023, 77.6% of it stock-related.'
      url: https://files.epi.org/uploads/288934.pdf

    text t65 "21 : 1" #b5482e at.data [1968,70] cites ["epi2024"] font_size 36 hidden true
    text t78 "31 : 1" #b5482e at.data [1979,95] font_size 36 hidden true
    text big "290 : 1" #b5482e cites ["epi2024"] x 870 y 560 font_size 64 hidden true
    text pay "$22m a year" #3d3833 cites ["epi2024"] x 870 y 360 font_size 34 hidden true
    text pay2 "78% in stock" #3d3833 x 870 y 305 font_size 34 hidden true
    text a1 "Contract?" #2f6b8f x 870 y 330 font_size 38 hidden true
    text a2 "Talent?" #2f6b8f x 870 y 270 font_size 38 hidden true
    text a3 "Skimming?" #2f6b8f x 870 y 210 font_size 38 hidden true
    text t00 "384 : 1" #b5482e at.data [2000,420] font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Why pay a CEO so much?"

Why pay a chief executive so much? In 1965, the boss of (@draw line_2@) a big American company was paid about twenty times what a typical work (@draw t65@)er in the same industry earned.
    draw axes

B: Twenty-one to one, by the Economic Policy Institute's count. And by 1978 (@draw t78@) it had crept up to thirty-one.

    ask "Draw the rest of the line, to 2023." on line_1 from 1978 store g default 60 stage own
        intro: Now you: draw where you think it went over the next forty-five years.
        right: About three hundred to one: it took off, the way you drew it.
        wrong: Much steeper than that: here, about three hundred to one, ten times 1978.

    erase t65 t78 line_2

B: Here's what happened. About sixty to one by 1989. Then, in the 1990s, it took off.
    highlight line_1

Three hundred and  (@draw t00@)eighty-four to one in 2000, and near four hundred again in 2021. In 2023, two hu (@draw big@)ndred and ninety to one.

    pause 0.5

    erase t00

B: That's the average boss of the 350 largest US firms: about tw (@draw pay@)enty-two million dollars in 2023, more than three quar (@draw pay2@)ters of it in stock.

    erase pay pay2

My first guess: they take it because they can.
    highlight big

B: Mine: a great boss is worth every penny. Which is it?
    highlight big effect circle

Economists give three answers: a co (@draw a1@)ntract problem, a marke (@draw a2@)t for talent, and plain skimm (@draw a3@)ing. Start with t (@highlight a1@)he contract.

    pause 0.5

## How do you pay someone you can't watch?
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 11.5
    'y':
      - 0
      - 12
    box:
      x: 70
      'y': 150
      w: 640
      h: 450
vars:
    S:
      value: 1
      min: 0
      max: 10
      label: Salary ($m)
    s:
      value: 0
      min: 0
      max: 100
      label: Stake (%)
    g:
      expr: 10*s/100
    eff:
      expr: 10*s/100
sources:
    - id: jm1990
      title: Performance Pay and Top-Management Incentives
      authors: Michael C. Jensen, Kevin J. Murphy
      year: 1990
      finding: 'Journal of Political Economy 98(2): 225–264. CEO wealth changes about $3.25 for every $1,000 change in shareholder wealth (pay, options, stockholdings and dismissal combined).'

    axes ax x_label "Her effort" y_label "$m per extra unit of effort" cross corner ticks.x false ticks.y [0,5,10] hidden true
    text pa "Principal: owners" #8a5fa8 x 850 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true
    text pa2 "Agent: the CEO" #8a5fa8 x 850 y 510 font_size 30 hidden true
    curve worth dashed thick #2f6b8f expr "10" x_from 0 x_to 11 hidden true
    text l_worth "Worth to owners: $10m" #2f6b8f at.data [3.2,10.9] font_size 28 hidden true
    curve cost thick #3d3833 expr x x_from 0 x_to 11.3 hidden true
    text l_cost "Cost to her" at.data [8.2,5.6] font_size 28 hidden true
    dot ideal at.on cost at.x 10 hidden true
    label l_ideal "Owner's ideal" below-right attach_to ideal hidden true
    curve gain #b5482e expr g x_from 0 x_to 11 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    dot dot #b5482e at.on cost at.x 0 bind {"at.x":"eff"} hidden true
    label l_dot "She stops here" above-left #b5482e attach_to dot hidden true
    region agency x_from.ref dot x_to.ref ideal between ["worth","cost"] style.fill #c96567 style.opacity 0.45 hidden true
    text l_agency "Agency cost" #b5482e at.data [6.3,8.3] font_size 30 hidden true
    text r_sal "Salary: ${S:1}m" #3d3833 x 860 y 560 font_size 32 hidden true
    text r_stake "Stake: {s:1}%" #b5482e x 860 y 500 font_size 32 hidden true
    text r_gain "Her gain: ${g:2}m\nper unit" #b5482e x 860 y 420 font_size 28 hidden true
    text jm "$3.25\nper $1,000" #b5482e cites ["jm1990"] x 860 y 290 font_size 40 hidden true
    text bur "“paid like\nbureaucrats”" #8a5fa8 x 860 y 180 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "How do you pay someone you can't watch?"

B: A big company belongs to thousands of shareholders, who never see the boss at work. It's Berle and Means's split of ownership and control, and it's where the trouble starts.
    draw ax

Economists call it a principal–agent p (@draw pa@)roblem: the principal wants a  (@draw pa2@)job done, the agent does it, and the principal can't watch how hard the agent tries.

    erase pa pa2

B: Say, to put numbers on it, each extra unit of  (@draw worth l_worth@)the boss's effort adds ten million dollars to what the firm is worth.

But effort costs her: l (@draw cost l_cost@)onger weeks, hard calls, firing old friends. And each extra unit costs her more than the last.

B: If she owned the whole firm, she'd work up to here (@draw ideal l_ideal@), where the last unit costs her just what it adds.

Now pay her a fixed salary. Does a bigger salary make her try harder?
    draw r_sal r_stake r_gain gain dot l_dot

B: Not in this model. She gets it however hard she works, so the extra effort earns her nothing. Beyond what pride or fear of the sack already buys, she coasts.
    animate S 5 duration 3

Give her a stake inst (@highlight r_stake@)ead: a share of every dollar she adds. At (@animate s 10 duration 3@) ten percent, her gain is a million dollars a unit, and her effort follows.

B: The gap between her effo (@draw agency l_agency@)rt and the owners' ideal is the agency cost: value that nobody creates.

In 1990, Michael Jensen and Kevin Murphy measured the real stake: when shareholders gained a thous (@draw jm@)and dollars, the (@animate s 0.325 duration 2.5@) boss gained three dollars and twenty-five cents.

B: In this bare model that barely moves her. Big-company bosses, t (@draw bur@)hey concluded, were paid like bureaucrats.

    erase jm bur

    highlight r_sal r_stake

Try it: mix a salary and a stake, and watch which one moves her effort.
    explore params ["S","s"] watch "The salary swings from ten million to nothing and her effort stays put; then the stake swings, and her effort rides with it."

B: Whatever the mix, only the stake moved her. Pile on salary and the dot sits still.
    animate S 5 s 10 duration 1

## Bigger firms, bigger pay
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 1965
      - 1973
      - 1978
      - 1989
      - 1995
      - 2000
      - 2007
      - 2009
      - 2022
      - 2023
    series:
      - {name: CEO pay, color: '#b5482e', points: true, values: [56, 73, 100, 187, 362, 1310, 1185, 621, 1470, 1185]}
      - {name: Stock market, color: '#2f6b8f', values: [180, 160, 100, 187, 262, 617, 542, 338, 1104, 1110]}
      - {name: Workers' pay, color: '#8f887c', values: [82, 98, 100, 95, 95, 102, 107, 112, 123, 124]}
    stage: 0
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 1600
    xlim:
      - 1965
      - 2023
    y_label: Index, 1978 = 100 (after inflation)
    x_label: Year
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 90
      w: 600
      h: 540
sources:
    - id: epi2024
      title: 'CEO pay declined in 2023 (Table 1: CEO compensation, CEO-to-worker ratio and stock prices, 2023$)'
      authors: Josh Bivens, Elise Gould, Jori Kandra (Economic Policy Institute)
      year: 2024
      finding: '1978–2023: realized CEO pay +1,085%, S&P 500 (real) +1,008%, typical worker compensation +24%. Indexed here to 1978 = 100.'
      url: https://files.epi.org/uploads/288934.pdf
    - id: hl1998
      title: Are CEOs Really Paid Like Bureaucrats?
      authors: Brian J. Hall, Jeffrey B. Liebman
      year: 1998
      finding: 'Quarterly Journal of Economics 113(3): 653–691. From 1980 to 1994, pay-performance sensitivity rose by factors of 2 to nearly 7, mostly through stock option grants.'
    - id: gl2008
      title: Why Has CEO Pay Increased So Much?
      authors: Xavier Gabaix, Augustin Landier
      year: 2008
      finding: 'Quarterly Journal of Economics 123(1): 49–100. In a talent-assignment model, CEO pay rises with the market value of large firms; the sixfold rise in US CEO pay 1980–2003 matches the sixfold rise in large firms'' market capitalisation.'

    text hl "1980–94:\npay–value link\n×2 to ×7" #8a5fa8 cites ["hl1998"] x 880 y 560 font_size 34 hidden true
    text t1185 "≈ 1,185" #b5482e cites ["epi2024"] x 880 y 590 font_size 48 hidden true
    text t11 "Stocks ×11\nWorkers +24%" #2f6b8f cites ["epi2024"] x 880 y 370 font_size 38 hidden true
    text risk "Shares = risk\n→ more pay" #3d3833 x 880 y 370 font_size 38 hidden true
    text talent "Market\nfor talent" #2f6b8f x 880 y 370 font_size 44 hidden true
    text edge "1% × $500bn\n= $5bn" #3d3833 x 880 y 220 font_size 38 hidden true
    text six "1980–2003:\npay ×6\nfirm value ×6" #2f6b8f cites ["gl2008"] x 880 y 220 font_size 36 hidden true
    text price "A price,\nnot theft?" #2f6b8f x 880 y 370 font_size 46 hidden true

    card title "Bigger firms, bigger pay"

Boards took the lesson. By 1994, Brian Hall and Jeffrey Liebman found the link between bosses' pay and (@draw hl@) their firms' value had grown several times stronger, mostly through stock options.
    draw axes

    erase hl

B: And here's what happened to the pay itself, after inflation, with 1978 set at a hundred. By 2023 it's ne (@draw t1185@)ar twelve hundred.
    draw line_1

    quiz "What else rose almost as fast as CEO pay?" judge false
        intro: Guess what else rose almost as fast.
        right: Here it comes.
        * Typical workers' pay
        * The stock market

B: The st (@draw line_2@)ock market. The S&P 500, after inflation, rose abo (@draw t11@)ut elevenfold. Ty (@draw line_3@)pical workers' pay: up about a quarter.

    erase t11

So part of it is mechanical. Pay someone in shares, and her pay rises when the shares do.
    highlight line_1 line_2

B: And shares are risky. A boss with her wealth tied to one f (@draw risk@)irm wants extra pay for carrying that risk. Aligning interests raises pay all by itself.

    erase risk

Xavier Gabaix and Augustin Landier give a deep (@draw talent@)er reason: a market for talent.

B: The scarce best bosses go to the biggest firms, where a small edge is worth the most. One perce (@draw edge@)nt better decisions at a firm worth five hundred billion dollars: five billion. Illustrative, but that's the logic.
    highlight talent

    erase edge

In their model, bosses' pay grows with the value of large firms. Be (@draw six@)tween 1980 and 2003, both rose about sixfold.

    erase t1185 talent six

B: So on that view, pay rose because t (@draw price@)he firms grew. Not t (@highlight line_1 line_2@)heft: a price.

    pause 0.5

## Talent, or skimming?
sources:
    - id: bm2001
      title: Are CEOs Rewarded for Luck? The Ones Without Principals Are
      authors: Marianne Bertrand, Sendhil Mullainathan
      year: 2001
      finding: 'Quarterly Journal of Economics 116(3): 901–932. CEO pay responds to luck (e.g. oil-price changes for oil firms) about as much as to general performance; firms with a large shareholder pay less for luck.'
    - id: bf2004
      title: 'Pay Without Performance: The Unfulfilled Promise of Executive Compensation'
      authors: Lucian Bebchuk, Jesse Fried
      year: 2004
      finding: 'Managerial-power view: executives influence the boards that set their pay; the main limit is ''outrage'' costs, so pay is camouflaged rather than set at arm''s length.'
    - id: epi2024
      title: CEO pay declined in 2023
      authors: Josh Bivens, Elise Gould, Jori Kandra (Economic Policy Institute)
      year: 2024
      finding: 'CEO compensation relative to top 0.1% wage earners: 2.6 on average in 1965–1978, 9.4 in 2022.'
      url: https://files.epi.org/uploads/288934.pdf
    - id: kaplan
      title: Are CEOs Overpaid? The Case Against (Chicago Booth Review)
      authors: Chicago Booth Review, on Steven N. Kaplan
      year: 2015
      finding: Kaplan argues CEO pay has moved in line with other top earners such as lawyers and private-company executives, and that adding private-equity-owned firms challenges the pay-for-luck thesis.
      url: https://www.chicagobooth.edu/review/are-ceos-overpaid
    - id: ffmm2013
      title: Are U.S. CEOs Paid More? New International Evidence
      authors: Nuno Fernandes, Miguel A. Ferreira, Pedro Matos, Kevin J. Murphy
      year: 2013
      finding: 'Review of Financial Studies 26(2): 323–367. Across 14 countries, the US CEO pay premium is economically modest and mostly reflects performance-based (equity) pay.'
takeaways:
    - Contract — makes her care
    - Talent — big firms bid
    - Skimming — she sets it

    text h_tal "Market for talent" #2f6b8f x 270 y 560 font_size 48 hidden true
    text h_skim "Skimming" #b5482e x 730 y 560 font_size 48 hidden true
    text claim "Pay follows\nfirm value" #2f6b8f x 270 y 430 font_size 44 hidden true
    text luck "…or luck?" #b5482e x 270 y 310 font_size 44 hidden true
    text oil "Oil price ↑\n→ oil bosses' pay ↑" #b5482e cites ["bm2001"] x 730 y 430 font_size 38 hidden true
    text owner "Big owner watching\n→ less pay for luck" #b5482e cites ["bm2001"] x 730 y 430 font_size 38 hidden true
    text board "She helps pick\nthe board.\nLimit: outrage" #b5482e cites ["bf2004"] x 730 y 430 font_size 38 hidden true
    text topn "CEO ÷ top 0.1%:\n2.6 → 9.4" #b5482e cites ["epi2024"] x 730 y 430 font_size 42 hidden true
    text kap "Kaplan: the whole\ntop rose" #2f6b8f cites ["kaplan"] x 270 y 200 font_size 38 hidden true
    cards sortc check each hidden true
        Market for talent: Pay rose with firm value
        Skimming: Oil bosses paid for oil-price luck, Less pay for luck where a big owner watches, CEOs pulled away from the top 0.1%
        Market for talent: Lawyers' and private bosses' pay rose too
    text st1 "Stakes change effort:\nmostly agreed" #2f6b8f x 500 y 540 font_size 48 hidden true
    text st2 "Why pay rose so much:\ncontested" #b5482e cites ["ffmm2013"] x 500 y 390 font_size 48 hidden true
    text intl "14 countries: US lead modest,\nmostly pay in shares" #3d3833 cites ["ffmm2013"] x 500 y 250 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Talent, or skimming?"

But there's a catch in that chart. The stock market rises for reasons no boss (@draw luck@) controls: falling interest rates, a boom, a bubble.
    draw h_tal claim

    fade claim to 0.35

B: The same pair, Bertrand and Mullainathan, tested exactly that in 2001. When the oil price rose, oil (@draw oil@) company bosses were paid more, for luck they did nothing to earn.
    draw h_skim

    erase oil

And where a big sha (@draw owner@)reholder kept watch, the pay for luck was smaller. That's (@highlight h_skim@) the skimming view.

    erase owner

B: Lucian Bebchuk and Jesse Fried put it pla (@draw board@)inly: the boss helps choose the board that sets her pay, and the real limit is public outrage.

    erase board

The Economic Policy Institute adds that bosses pulled away even from oth (@draw topn@)er top earners: from under three times the top 0.1 percent's pay in 1965 to 1978, to over nine times in 2022.

B: Steven Kaplan replies that the  (@draw kap@)whole top rose: lawyers and private-company executives, alongside the CEOs. Sort the findings: which side does each one help?

    erase h_tal h_skim claim luck topn kap

    draw sortc

    ask "Which side does each finding help? Sort them." on sortc store f stage own voice b
        right: All five. Luck, and how well the owners watch, point to skimming; pay tracking firm value and the rest of the top points to talent.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total}: three skimming, two talent.

How sure are we? That a stake in the firm changes (@draw st1@) what a boss does: mostly agreed.

B: Why the pay rose this mu (@draw st2@)ch: contested. Even the American lead is disputed: one study of fourtee (@draw intl@)n countries found it modest, and mostly the extra pay in shares.

    erase intl

    quiz "A hospital board pays its director a bonus whenever the region's population grows. What would the skimming view say?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: It pays for luck: the director can do little about how many people move to the region.
        wrong: Ask what the director can do about how many people move to the region: here, it pays for luck.
        + It pays for luck, not effort
        * It's a sharp incentive
        * It's too small to matter

    quiz "A café owner hires a manager, and sees every sale and every shift live on her phone. Does she need to give the manager a profit share?" voice b
        right: Less so: a stake fixes effort nobody can see, and she can see it.
        wrong: A stake is there because the owner can't watch the effort: here, she can, so less need.
        + Less so: she can watch, so salary and checking will do
        * Yes, a bigger share than a CEO gets
        * Yes, the same share a CEO gets

    quiz "Say a sports league's TV money triples in ten years, and the best coaches are no better than before. What does the talent view predict for their pay?"
        right: It rises a lot: the same edge now wins far more money, so clubs bid more for it.
        wrong: Ask what one more win is now worth to a club: here, coaches' pay rises a lot.
        + It rises a lot
        * It stays the same: same skill
        * It falls

    erase st1 st2

So why pay a chief executive so much? Partly to make h (@draw takeaway_1@)er care about the owners' money, partly beca (@draw takeaway_2@)use big firms bid for a few bosses, and partly because (@draw takeaway_3@) she helps set her own pay.

When a boss's pay hits the headlines, the questions to ask are what her stake rewards, who else was bidding for her, and who chose the board that set it.

B: Firms exist to save on contracts, and at the very top the hardest contract of all is still being written.
    highlight takeaway_1

    pause 0.5

