      ms: 12120
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1so7tqh'
    '|a||Give her a stake instead: a share of every dollar she adds. At ten percent, her gain is a million dollars a unit, and her effort follows.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9480
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:i4oetg'
    '|b||The gap between her effort and the owners'' ideal is the agency cost: value that nobody creates.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7120
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:m1p2dd'
    '|a||In 1990, Michael Jensen and Kevin Murphy measured the real stake: when shareholders gained a thousand dollars, the boss gained three dollars and twenty-five cents.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9840
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:19ks1n5'
    '|b||In this bare model that barely moves her. Big-company bosses, they concluded, were paid like bureaucrats.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6360
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:144el3b'
    '|a||Try it: mix a salary and a stake, and watch which one moves her effort.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5184
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||Boards took the lesson. By 1994, Brian Hall and Jeffrey Liebman found the link between bosses'' pay and their firms'' value had grown several times stronger, mostly through stock options.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12360
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||And here''s what happened to the pay itself, after inflation, with 1978 set at a hundred. By 2023 it''s near twelve hundred.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8800
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:5iqiko'
    '|a||Guess what else rose almost as fast. What else rose almost as fast as CEO pay?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5800
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:jak9b8'
    '|a||Here it comes.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 1680
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||The stock market. The S&P 500, after inflation, rose about elevenfold. Typical workers'' pay: up about a quarter.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8440
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||So part of it is mechanical. Pay someone in shares, and her pay rises when the shares do.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5800
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:g57emv'
    '|b||And shares are risky. A boss with her wealth tied to one firm wants extra pay for carrying that risk. Aligning interests raises pay all by itself.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8920
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:b1dyf9'
    '|a||Xavier Gabaix and Augustin Landier give a deeper reason: a market for talent.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5680
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1oix4uh'
    '|b||The scarce best bosses go to the biggest firms, where a small edge is worth the most. One percent better decisions at a firm worth five hundred billion dollars: five billion. Illustrative, but that''s the logic.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 13360
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:4y2sfm'
    '|a||In their model, bosses'' pay grows with the value of large firms. Between 1980 and 2003, both rose about sixfold.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9000
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:v8ur25'
    '|b||So on that view, pay rose because the firms grew. Not theft: a price.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5280
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1heq0dx'
    '|a||But there''s a catch in that chart. The stock market rises for reasons no boss controls: falling interest rates, a boom, a bubble.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9400
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||The same pair, Bertrand and Mullainathan, tested exactly that in 2001. When the oil price rose, oil company bosses were paid more, for luck they did nothing to earn.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11160
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1xddpi5'
    '|a||And where a big shareholder kept watch, the pay for luck was smaller. That''s the skimming view.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6000
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:12h9ja2'
    '|b||Lucian Bebchuk and Jesse Fried put it plainly: the boss helps choose the board that sets her pay, and the real limit is public outrage.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8720
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1i2mg69'
    '|a||The Economic Policy Institute adds that bosses pulled away even from other top earners: from under three times the top 0.1 percent''s pay in 1965 to 1978, to over nine times in 2022.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 13920
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:15m0twq'
    '|b||Steven Kaplan replies that the whole top rose: lawyers and private-company executives, alongside the CEOs. Sort the findings: which side does each one help?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10120
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:kr11py'
    '|b||Which side does each finding help? Sort them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3200
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:197g2uj'
    '|a||How sure are we? That a stake in the firm changes what a boss does: mostly agreed.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5800
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||Why the pay rose this much: contested. Even the American lead is disputed: one study of fourteen countries found it modest, and mostly the extra pay in shares.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9880
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:9318bu'
    '|a||Three new cases. A hospital board pays its director a bonus whenever the region''s population grows. What would the skimming view say?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8400
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:u6q5rf'
    '|a||It pays for luck: the director can do little about how many people move to the region.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5600
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||A café owner hires a manager, and sees every sale and every shift live on her phone. Does she need to give the manager a profit share?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9360
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|b||Less so: a stake fixes effort nobody can see, and she can see it.':
